---
import Try from "../Try.astro";
import "../sternzeit/sternzeit.css";
import "./dunstkreis.css";
import "./stages.css";

// The five stages from the tables' light to a pixel, each with its one control.
const STAGES = [
    { name: "Four wavelengths", note: "630, 580, 510 and 440 nm, clockwise from top left" },
    { name: "Linear RGB", note: "the brightest sky at white" },
    { name: "Exposed", note: "clipped at white" },
    { name: "Tone mapped", note: "folded into white" },
    { name: "The pixel", note: "sRGB encoded, quantized" },
];
---

<figure class="scene-frame wide stages" id="stages">
    <div class="stages-view">
        <canvas data-field="canvas" aria-label="A patch of sky around the Sun after each stage from light to pixel"></canvas>
    </div>
    <div class="stages-row">
        {
            STAGES.map(({ name, note }, i) => (
                <div class="stages-cell">
                    <p class="stages-name">
                        {i + 1}. {name}
                    </p>
                    <p class="stages-note">{note}</p>
                    {i === 0 && (
                        <span class="stages-control" role="radiogroup" aria-label="Matrix">
                            <button type="button" class="view-toggle" data-matrix="fitted" aria-pressed="true" title="The four wavelengths to RGB by the matrix fitted to sky spectra">fitted</button>
                            <button type="button" class="view-toggle" data-matrix="samples" aria-pressed="false" title="The first three wavelengths taken as R, G and B, the fourth left out">as RGB</button>
                        </span>
                    )}
                    {i === 1 && (
                        <span class="stages-control" role="radiogroup" aria-label="Gamut">
                            <button type="button" class="view-toggle" data-gamut="map" aria-pressed="true" title="A color outside sRGB desaturated towards its own gray until it fits">mapped</button>
                            <button type="button" class="view-toggle" data-gamut="clip" aria-pressed="false" title="A channel below zero cut off at zero">clipped</button>
                        </span>
                    )}
                    {i === 2 && (
                        <label class="stages-control stages-ev">
                            EV<sub>100</sub> <input data-field="ev" type="range" min="-2" max="18" step="0.1" value="12" /> <output data-field="evValue" />
                        </label>
                    )}
                    {i === 3 && (
                        <span class="stages-control" role="radiogroup" aria-label="Tone curve">
                            <button type="button" class="view-toggle" data-curve="1" aria-pressed="true">Neutral</button>
                            <button type="button" class="view-toggle" data-curve="2" aria-pressed="false">AgX</button>
                            <button type="button" class="view-toggle" data-curve="3" aria-pressed="false">ACES</button>
                        </span>
                    )}
                    {i === 4 && (
                        <span class="stages-control">
                            <span class="stages-control" role="radiogroup" aria-label="Bits per channel">
                                <button type="button" class="view-toggle" data-bits="8" aria-pressed="true" title="Eight bits per channel, what a screen is sent">8 bits</button>
                                <button type="button" class="view-toggle" data-bits="4" aria-pressed="false" title="Four bits per channel, never used for real: sixteen times coarser than eight, only so the bands eight bits leave in a smooth gradient show">4<span class="stages-wide">, exaggerated</span></button>
                            </span>
                            <button type="button" class="view-toggle" data-field="dither" aria-pressed="true" title="A step of noise against the bands a smooth gradient falls into">dither</button>
                        </span>
                    )}
                </div>
            ))
        }
    </div>
    <div class="stages-transfer">
        <svg class="stages-plot" aria-label="The light along the slice mapped to code values: the curve from cd/m² to the pixel, with the slice's values as dots and histograms along both axes"></svg>
    </div>
    <label class="figure-slider" data-offset title="The moment, up to half a day either way from where it was set: drag, or turn the mouse wheel for ten minutes a notch">
        <span class="note">Time</span>
        <span class="figure-slider-rail">
            <input type="range" data-field="offset" min="-720" max="720" step="5" value="0" aria-label="Minutes from the moment" />
            <span class="figure-slider-scale" aria-hidden="true">{["−12", "0", "+12"].map((mark, i) => <span class="figure-slider-mark" style={`left: ${i * 50}%`}>{mark}{i !== 1 && <span class="figure-slider-unit">{" h"}</span>}</span>)}</span>
        </span>
        <output data-field="offsetValue" />
    </label>
    <figcaption class="figure-caption">
        One patch of the sky around the Sun, from the light in the tables to a pixel on the screen, each stage with its one
        choice, and under it the sky straight below and above the Sun, from the horizon to the zenith, from light to code
        value. Its luminance in cd/m² runs across on a logarithmic scale and the code value it is written as up, both
        counted along the edges in bars of equal width. The curves are the chain for a gray light: the exposure moves it
        sideways, the sRGB transfer curve lifts the darks, dashed, above the light written linearly, dotted, and the tone
        curve rolls off the highlights. The dots are the slice every degree in its own colors, each at the code value of
        a gray as bright as its pixel: a gray light lies on the curve, a colored one off it by what the tone curve does to
        its color.
        Every choice is passed on: clip the gamut, and the exposed, tone mapped and encoded sky inherit it. Four bits are
        never used; they only magnify the bands that eight bits leave in a smooth gradient.
        <Try label="The setting Sun" set="date=2026-04-06T17:50:00Z&lat=52.395&lon=13.085&h=1" fig="stages">
            The sky around it lies outside sRGB, hatched. Take the wavelengths as RGB instead, and the sunset pales.
        </Try>
    </figcaption>
</figure>

<script>
    import "./stages";
</script>
